00:45:52.120 --> 00:45:53.560
Oh, I thought you meant like today.

00:45:53.900 --> 00:45:54.980
Yeah. Oh, no, no, no.

00:45:55.060 --> 00:45:56.440
Nobody can talk to the dead.

00:45:57.380 --> 00:45:58.960
I thought you were implying that Napoleon was still alive.

00:45:58.960 --> 00:46:00.960
And that's just like personal finance advice, too.

00:46:00.960 --> 00:46:05.160
Like, if you're spending money to talk to anybody who's dead, stop immediately.

00:46:05.560 --> 00:46:06.700
Like, they're not doing it.

00:46:06.800 --> 00:46:08.240
We're not trying to tell you how to live your lives.

00:46:08.360 --> 00:46:19.840
If you do know how to commune with the dead, if you do know a psychic or a medium that isn't lying, tell us.

00:46:19.980 --> 00:46:23.120
Yeah, if they're proficient in lottery numbers, that's great, too.

00:46:23.140 --> 00:46:28.300
I would love to know where the S&P 500 will be on August 13th.

00:46:28.300 --> 00:46:30.740
So we are going to end the episode here.

00:46:31.060 --> 00:46:33.940
But just a quick note that we have a fun post-show coming up.

00:46:34.020 --> 00:46:35.480
So maybe you'll listen to that one as well.

00:46:35.520 --> 00:46:37.620
I had no idea. Yeah, you guys have no idea.

00:46:37.680 --> 00:46:38.560
I'm excited. We're about to do it.

00:46:38.740 --> 00:46:40.040
Thank you so much. I'm Michael Spito.

00:46:40.120 --> 00:46:41.560
You can find me on at Esposito Film on Twitter.

00:46:41.700 --> 00:46:44.400
You can find Nostalgia on at NostalgiaCast on Twitter.

00:46:44.680 --> 00:46:45.680
Tell your friends all about it.

00:46:45.780 --> 00:46:46.820
We'll talk to you soon. Goodbye.

00:46:47.220 --> 00:47:12.580
Bye. All right, guys.

00:47:12.680 --> 00:47:14.260
Very exciting news for this post-show.

00:47:14.500 --> 00:47:15.720
All right? All right.

00:47:15.940 --> 00:47:22.340
We are now in September of 1988, and we have missed the very first issue of Nintendo Power

00:47:22.340 --> 00:47:26.900
coming out. It came out in July and August of 88.

00:47:27.500 --> 00:47:39.040
And something I'd like to kind of incorporate into the post shows at least is going through the magazine as it comes out month by month and just reading the fan mail and responding to it as if where are those people in the fan mail.

00:47:39.420 --> 00:47:45.580
So there's this section called the mailbox at the end of Nintendo Power.

00:47:45.720 --> 00:47:52.000
It's after they do the players forum section, which is just them saying, look, celebrities play NES.

00:47:52.000 --> 00:47:53.820
Yes, that's usually a photo op thing.

00:47:55.000 --> 00:48:00.740
So after they're done shilling, they get to this part where they answer people's burning questions.

00:48:01.180 --> 00:48:04.280
These aren't really questions, but I'd still like you guys to respond to them.

00:48:04.380 --> 00:48:05.820
These are just things that they've written in.

00:48:06.080 --> 00:48:12.300
Okay. So this first one comes from Michael Johnson in Durham, North Carolina.

00:48:12.640 --> 00:48:14.480
Durham. Not Durham.

00:48:14.900 --> 00:48:16.560
That's important to note. I'll do that in the future.

00:48:17.280 --> 00:48:18.480
He says,

00:48:18.860 --> 00:48:25.500
If you're like I am, that is crazy about martial arts, the legend of Kage is the game for you.

00:48:25.760 --> 00:48:30.360
I don't see how they get all this fun and adventure on one game pack.

00:48:30.540 --> 00:48:40.480
In the game, you are Kage, a young ninja warrior with one dangerous task, to save the shogun's daughter, Princess Kiri from the clutches of the evil warlord Yuki.

00:48:40.480 --> 00:48:46.160
On your journey, you will encounter evil ninjas, fire-breathing monks, and even Yuki's right-hand man, Yoshi.

00:48:46.680 --> 00:48:54.480
You are armed with two weapons, your ninja sword and a limitless supply of star knives that you can be hurled at your opponents like stones.

00:48:54.960 --> 00:48:58.300
If you wish to be reunited with your beloved Kiri, you must prevail.

00:48:58.760 --> 00:49:00.860
Sound fun? Think you have what it takes?

00:49:01.220 --> 00:49:04.340
Purchase the legend of Kage, a game you will treasure.

00:49:04.540 --> 00:49:05.740
Wait, my fan wrote this in?

00:49:05.740 --> 00:49:08.380
That's not them being done shilling.

00:49:08.600 --> 00:49:09.460
That is not a fan.

00:49:09.820 --> 00:49:12.600
You don't think that Michael Johnson wrote that?

00:49:12.800 --> 00:49:15.520
I don't think it's hilarious that that's so transparent.

00:49:15.800 --> 00:49:22.620
I think it's so funny that he also loves this game that we just dunked on so bad in that episode.

00:49:22.620 --> 00:49:24.840
I just remember being confused when I put it up that game.

00:49:24.840 --> 00:49:27.120
Yeah, you have to catch a butterfly or something at one point.

00:49:27.120 --> 00:49:38.180
Yeah, and there were also like the lightning strikes with the wood, trees everywhere, and you just jump in leaps of faith throughout the air to other ninjas and have to take them out.

00:49:38.240 --> 00:49:46.460
I just think that Michael Johnson was a ghost name for the president of Nintendo Power, and that was nothing.

00:49:46.640 --> 00:49:48.400
That was not a question.

00:49:48.740 --> 00:49:52.460
I don't know. Who could make up a name like Michael Johnson?

00:49:52.860 --> 00:49:57.700
I don't know. There couldn't have possibly really been any questions for the first issue.

00:49:57.880 --> 00:49:59.040
How do you prepare people for that?

00:49:59.060 --> 00:50:00.140
That's true. Like what kind of...

00:50:00.140 --> 00:50:04.500
I would figure that there'd just be people writing into Nintendo and asking them questions like,

00:50:04.620 --> 00:50:05.780
can I work on your next game?

00:50:05.900 --> 00:50:07.880
Right. Do you know when Super Mario 2 is coming out?

00:50:07.880 --> 00:50:11.980
It'd be funny if someone wrote into the magazine, can I work on your next game, and they just answered in the magazine, no.

00:50:13.560 --> 00:50:18.340
Okay, so this one comes from the Videoizers from Massachusetts.

00:50:19.200 --> 00:50:22.420
My friends and I have a club called the Videoizers.

00:50:23.040 --> 00:50:25.520
Here are a few suggestions for some other clubs.

00:50:25.720 --> 00:50:28.900
Try saving up weekly for a new tape every month.

00:50:29.080 --> 00:50:31.320
So far, our club has 13 tapes.

00:50:31.580 --> 00:50:37.180
In our club, we save a few dollars a week for extra money to call the Nintendo Hotline.

00:50:37.380 --> 00:50:42.040
A friendly game counselor kindly helps us out when we have trouble on something.

00:50:42.380 --> 00:50:44.300
Wow. That was an action...

00:50:44.300 --> 00:50:47.420
They paid for tips?

00:50:48.400 --> 00:50:49.280
You didn't know about this.

00:50:49.440 --> 00:50:50.280
I didn't know about that.

00:50:50.300 --> 00:50:51.660
I knew about the tip. Okay, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:50:51.820 --> 00:50:54.160
The Nintendo Hotline was actually done by...

00:50:54.160 --> 00:50:56.800
You had to pay. You would pay by the minute.

00:50:57.400 --> 00:50:58.700
And you would tell them...

00:50:58.700 --> 00:51:00.600
So how long would they take to give you the answer?

00:51:01.260 --> 00:51:03.380
Would they draw it out? I think they probably would try to take long.

00:51:03.560 --> 00:51:07.240
But basically, you could be at any point in any game, right?

00:51:07.240 --> 00:51:10.400
Call them while you're there and ask for help.

00:51:10.680 --> 00:51:14.180
And then they were supposed to be able to help you right there.

00:51:14.280 --> 00:51:16.020
Be like, oh, we got to do this thing.

00:51:16.220 --> 00:51:18.020
Or try jumping over that.

00:51:18.320 --> 00:51:19.980
Or press these combinations of buttons.

00:51:20.200 --> 00:51:23.580
It was just like the original game FAQs, I guess.

00:51:23.840 --> 00:51:30.300
But I actually did this one time with my mom for a game called Mischief Makers on the N64,

00:51:30.320 --> 00:51:31.620
which is now considered a classic.

00:51:31.620 --> 00:51:33.300
I hated the damn game.

00:51:33.520 --> 00:51:34.980
But we were stuck at a point.

00:51:35.100 --> 00:51:37.080
Like, all of us. We couldn't wrap our heads around it.

00:51:37.560 --> 00:51:42.640
And my mom called into, I think, the Toys R Us hotline because they had one as well.

00:51:42.700 --> 00:51:44.700
Really? And we bought the game from them.

00:51:44.700 --> 00:51:48.000
So maybe there was like a free thing for a limited time.

00:51:48.560 --> 00:51:50.320
And the guy could not help us over the phone.

00:51:50.420 --> 00:51:52.720
He had no idea. He just could not figure it out.

00:51:52.880 --> 00:51:58.640
He's like, people usually call me about Super Mario 64 or Wave Race 64 or Star Fox 64.

00:51:58.840 --> 00:52:00.920
Your game doesn't even have 64 in the title.

00:52:02.580 --> 00:52:05.640
But also, what do you think about this guy calling cartridges tapes?

00:52:06.460 --> 00:52:09.000
I figure he was actually talking about tapes.

00:52:09.420 --> 00:52:11.300
Yeah, that's what I thought, too, until you said that right now.

00:52:11.620 --> 00:52:13.480
Like cassette tapes. I just don't know.

00:52:13.520 --> 00:52:15.260
Why would he share that idea with Nintendo?

00:52:15.260 --> 00:52:20.300
I mean, why would somebody contact Nintendo to shill Legend of Kage?

00:52:20.500 --> 00:52:25.000
Like, I think it's just people from the 80s being goofy.

00:52:25.280 --> 00:52:26.500
So you think that the videoizers.

00:52:26.840 --> 00:52:29.780
The videoizers. Maybe they're talking about VHS or Betamax.

00:52:30.060 --> 00:52:33.380
Yeah, okay. VHS tapes were around in the late 80s.

00:52:33.400 --> 00:52:36.040
That's true. Yeah. So maybe those are the tapes.

00:52:36.760 --> 00:52:37.920
It's the tapes. VHS tapes.

00:52:38.080 --> 00:52:39.280
We have to get a hold of the tapes.

00:52:39.340 --> 00:52:40.160
That's the only way to know.

00:52:40.400 --> 00:52:44.600
Yeah. Or I'll just have to go back in time and ask somebody in 1988, like, what do you call cartridges?

00:52:45.520 --> 00:52:48.020
Just like shake them. Like frantically and with urgency.

00:52:48.020 --> 00:52:49.820
And it'd be shocking if they said tapes.

00:52:50.160 --> 00:52:51.420
You know, because I just prompted them.

00:52:51.580 --> 00:52:52.440
They're like, what's the cartridge?

00:52:52.780 --> 00:52:54.940
Yeah. The Nixon Watergate tapes.

00:52:55.300 --> 00:52:57.420
Yes. The Watergate cartridges.

00:52:57.720 --> 00:52:59.000
That's actually what that guy was collecting.

00:52:59.220 --> 00:53:01.620
The videoizers collected the Nixon Watergate tapes.

00:53:01.620 --> 00:53:03.620
Oh, yeah. Oh, I've heard of them before.

00:53:03.780 --> 00:53:05.100
Yeah. All right.

00:53:05.180 --> 00:53:07.220
So we'll do more Nintendo Power in the future.

00:53:07.480 --> 00:53:10.280
I hope that you guys have more. Hopefully there are actual questions that I can respond to.

00:53:10.320 --> 00:53:16.240
Yeah, I hope you guys will continue to listen. Right now, the top games on Nintendo Power is July, August, 1988 issue.

00:53:16.640 --> 00:53:19.160
The top five games, number five, Kid Icarus.

00:53:19.500 --> 00:53:21.160
Number four, Super Mario Brothers.

00:53:21.580 --> 00:53:23.060
Number three, Metroid.

00:53:23.500 --> 00:53:27.040
Number two, Punch-Out.

00:53:27.320 --> 00:53:28.760
Okay. So what's number one?

00:53:29.180 --> 00:53:30.320
Legend of Zelda.

00:53:30.560 --> 00:53:31.440
Yes, absolutely.

00:53:31.440 --> 00:53:37.520
Absolutely. And in the next issue of Nintendo Power, we'll be looking forward to Castlevania II, Simon's

00:53:37.520 --> 00:53:41.460
Quest, Bayou Billy, and Bionic Commando.

00:53:42.100 --> 00:53:43.520
Sweet. All right.

00:53:43.940 --> 00:53:45.920
Thanks so much for listening. We're out.
